 The Business Plan Found a Bug in the Agent. 
 neatlogs measured every run. cfo.ai turned the runs into 18 months of cash. The model showed that the agent's own budget cap could sink the company, so the cap moved into the code. 
 neatlogs measured 64 runs, one cost spread Mean $0.0167 a run. One run in twenty costs $0.0508, and the worst cost $0.1014. drivers.csv, a trace per row 
 cfo.ai modeled 18 months from the traces Ari built three plans, Stripe fees, churn and six Scenarios. Every cost cell reads the measured drivers. Open the public model 
 The plan found Cash runs out in month 14 The agent capped a run at $0.50, so a Scale run could cost five times its $0.0997 share of the fee. Read the business plan 
 FirstRun fixed The plan sets the cap firstrun run --tier scale stops a run at $0.028. A live Free-tier run stopped at $0.0115 of its $0.0108 cap. See the change, PR #15 
 Cash on hand, November 2026 to April 2028 
 Three cfo.ai Scenarios with the same customers. Only the cost cap or the Scale price changes. 
 Base, plan-set cap Old $0.50 cap Scale at $529 
 The chart reads the cfo.ai figures from data/cfo-model.json . 
 Show the numbers 
 Raise Scale to $529 Scale margin 86.5%. Month 18 cash $21,293. 
 Halve Scale to 1,500 runs Keeps $299. Scale margin 87.7%. Month 18 cash $17,390. 
 Figures read back from cfo.ai on 2026-10-10. Model costs are Sonnet 5.5 list price, which Anthropic did not bill. Customer counts are assumptions until a team pays. 
 How FirstRun Compares. 
 Yes Partly or with setup No 
 Feature FirstRun Claude Code GitHub Actions GitHub Agents Custom workflow 
 Runs the quickstart as a new developer Every step runs in a fresh Docker container Runs on your machine. An optional OS sandbox limits files and network A fresh runner per job, but you script every step Works in its own Actions-powered VM, on your repo, not a new-user setup Only as clean as the environment you build 
 Plans steps from a docs URL The planner writes one step per command When you prompt it with the page You write each step Starts from an issue or task, not a docs page You write the parser 
 Improvises when a step fails Stands out Classifies the failure, patches, retries up to 3 times per step Iterates inside one session The job fails and stops Iterates in one session and pushes commits to its draft PR Only what you code 
 Proves the fix A second clean container must pass the whole quickstart Only if you ask for it Only if you script a second run Your repo's own tests and CI, not a clean-user rerun Only if you code it 
 Telemetry per run Stands out neatlogs spans for planner, executor and verifier. Measured cost of $0.02 per run OpenTelemetry metrics and events, such as cost and tokens. Set up through environment variables Logs and run history. No agent traces A session log you can read or stream. No cost trace you own You build it 
 Opens the docs PR After you approve once When you ask Only if you script it Opens a draft PR and asks you for review Only if you code it 
 Ease of use Stands out Paste a docs URL, then paste the prompt into your coding agent One prompt, but you define the test and the clean setup A YAML file per quickstart Assign an issue to Copilot Highest effort 
 Proof in public repos 4 upstream PRs, all open (see the record above) Not specific to docs Not specific to docs Not specific to docs Depends on your build 
 FirstRun reads a docs quickstart and runs every step in a clean container, the way a new developer would. It stops at the first failure and classifies it. It tries a fix up to 3 times, then proves the fix with a second clean run. It asks once before it opens a PR. 
 FirstRun opened 4 upstream docs PRs, and all are open today. The dbt DuckDB quickstart had a broken jafgen command (#10149) and no stated Python version (#10150). The Entire CLI installer put entire on a path that a clean shell lacks (entireio/cli#2724). The AccuKnox knoxctl page named 0.9.0 as the latest release while its installer fetched 0.9.58, and its APT steps assume curl , gnupg and sudo (accuknox/help#671). MkDocs and Dagster passed with no docs change, so a run also shows when docs hold up. 
 Every run is a neatlogs trace, so a bad run has a readable cause. A docs bug surfaces in a run instead of in a reader who leaves without filing an issue. The four PRs now wait for maintainer review. 
